    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about Reliability.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nReliability is the ability of a system to perform its intended function consistently over time.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Reliability accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Reliability is the ability of a system to perform its intended function consistently over time.\n2. Use a concrete case: Measure and improve how consistently the system performs its intended function under expected failures.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Reliability, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Reliability first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nMeasure and improve how consistently the system performs its intended function under expected failures.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Reliability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, measure and improve how consistently the system performs its intended function under expected failures. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Reliability, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
