    slug: "systemarchitecture-how-would-you-troubleshoot-a-problem-involving-reliability-127",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about Reliability.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Reliability misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Reliability. Reliability is the ability of a system to perform its intended function consistently over time.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Reliability: Reliability is the ability of a system to perform its intended function consistently over time.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Measure and improve how consistently the system performs its intended function under expected failures.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Reliability, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nMeasure and improve how consistently the system performs its intended function under expected failures.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Reliability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, measure and improve how consistently the system performs its intended function under expected failures. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
