    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nReliability is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Reliability, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Reliability gives you: Reliability is the ability of a system to perform its intended function consistently over time.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Reliability, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Measure and improve how consistently the system performs its intended function under expected failures.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nMeasure and improve how consistently the system performs its intended function under expected failures.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Reliability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, measure and improve how consistently the system performs its intended function under expected failures. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
