    slug: "systemarchitecture-what-is-fault-tolerance-131",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about Fault tolerance.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFault tolerance reduces the impact of component failures through redundancy, isolation, detection, or recovery.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Fault tolerance accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Fault tolerance reduces the impact of component failures through redundancy, isolation, detection, or recovery.\n2. Use a concrete case: Keep useful work available during a dependency failure through isolation, redundancy, graceful degradation, or recovery mechanisms.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Fault tolerance, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Fault tolerance first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nKeep useful work available during a dependency failure through isolation, redundancy, graceful degradation, or recovery mechanisms.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Fault tolerance when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, keep useful work available during a dependency failure through isolation, redundancy, graceful degradation, or recovery mechanisms. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Fault tolerance as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Fault tolerance.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Fault tolerance, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
