    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFault tolerance is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Fault tolerance, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Fault tolerance gives you: Fault tolerance reduces the impact of component failures through redundancy, isolation, detection, or recovery.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Fault tolerance, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Keep useful work available during a dependency failure through isolation, redundancy, graceful degradation, or recovery mechanisms.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nKeep useful work available during a dependency failure through isolation, redundancy, graceful degradation, or recovery mechanisms.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Fault tolerance when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, keep useful work available during a dependency failure through isolation, redundancy, graceful degradation, or recovery mechanisms. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Fault tolerance as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Fault tolerance.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
