    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCircuit breakers stop or limit calls to an unhealthy dependency after defined failure conditions so the caller can fail faster or use a fallback.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Circuit breakers accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Circuit breakers stop or limit calls to an unhealthy dependency after defined failure conditions so the caller can fail faster or use a fallback.\n2. Use a concrete case: If a dependency repeatedly fails, a circuit breaker can stop sending requests temporarily and fail fast while allowing periodic probes to see whether recovery occurred.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Circuit breakers, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Circuit breakers first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a dependency repeatedly fails, a circuit breaker can stop sending requests temporarily and fail fast while allowing periodic probes to see whether recovery occurred.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Circuit breakers when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a dependency repeatedly fails, a circuit breaker can stop sending requests temporarily and fail fast while allowing periodic probes to see whether recovery occurred. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Circuit breakers as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Circuit breakers.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Circuit breakers, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
