    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRetries repeat a failed operation when the failure is believed to be transient and the operation can safely be repeated.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Retries accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Retries repeat a failed operation when the failure is believed to be transient and the operation can safely be repeated.\n2. Use a concrete case: A client can retry a transient network failure with bounded exponential backoff, but it should not blindly retry validation failures or non-idempotent operations.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Retries, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Retries first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA client can retry a transient network failure with bounded exponential backoff, but it should not blindly retry validation failures or non-idempotent operations.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Retries when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a client can retry a transient network failure with bounded exponential backoff, but it should not blindly retry validation failures or non-idempotent operations. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Retries as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Retries.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Retries, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
