    shortDescription: "Frequently asked SystemArchitecture interview question about Queues.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Queues misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Queues. Queues buffer work between producers and consumers and can absorb bursts or decouple processing.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Queues: Queues buffer work between producers and consumers and can absorb bursts or decouple processing.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A background worker can consume queued jobs in order without making the user's request wait for the slow work.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Queues, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA background worker can consume queued jobs in order without making the user's request wait for the slow work.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Queues when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a background worker can consume queued jobs in order without making the user's request wait for the slow work. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming delivery means exactly once without defining the actual semantics.\n- Ignoring consumer lag or poison messages.\n- Forgetting that partitioning and key choice affect ordering and throughput.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
