    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Event driven architecture comes from the behavior it provides: Event-driven architecture communicates important state changes or facts as events that other components can consume. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Publish an order-created event so inventory, notifications, and analytics can react without synchronous calls to every subsystem.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Event-driven architecture communicates important state changes or facts as events that other components can consume.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Publish an order-created event so inventory, notifications, and analytics can react without synchronous calls to every subsystem.\n3. State the trade-off: The design can improve separation and independent change, but more boundaries also introduce coordination, deployment, and debugging complexity.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nPublish an order-created event so inventory, notifications, and analytics can react without synchronous calls to every subsystem.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Event driven architecture when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, publish an order-created event so inventory, notifications, and analytics can react without synchronous calls to every subsystem. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming delivery means exactly once without defining the actual semantics.\n- Ignoring consumer lag or poison messages.\n- Forgetting that partitioning and key choice affect ordering and throughput.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
