    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Event driven architecture are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Event-driven architecture communicates important state changes or facts as events that other components can consume.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Assuming delivery means exactly once without defining the actual semantics.\n- Ignoring consumer lag or poison messages.\n- Forgetting that partitioning and key choice affect ordering and throughput.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: Publish an order-created event so inventory, notifications, and analytics can react without synchronous calls to every subsystem.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nPublish an order-created event so inventory, notifications, and analytics can react without synchronous calls to every subsystem.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Event driven architecture when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, publish an order-created event so inventory, notifications, and analytics can react without synchronous calls to every subsystem. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming delivery means exactly once without defining the actual semantics.\n- Ignoring consumer lag or poison messages.\n- Forgetting that partitioning and key choice affect ordering and throughput.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Event driven architecture. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
