    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Disaster recovery when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Disaster recovery.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Disaster recovery prepares the system and team to restore service and data after major failures.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A recovery plan should specify what can be lost, how quickly service must return, where backups live, and how recovery is tested.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Disaster recovery, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA recovery plan should specify what can be lost, how quickly service must return, where backups live, and how recovery is tested.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production system may use Disaster recovery when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a recovery plan should specify what can be lost, how quickly service must return, where backups live, and how recovery is tested. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Disaster recovery as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Disaster recovery.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
