    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Funnel in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: For example, in a customer opportunity, an engineer may run into this concept when a sales funnel represents stages through which prospects progress from awareness or qualification toward purchase and retention. The implementation can then be checked against the expected behavior.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: A sales funnel represents stages through which prospects progress from awareness or qualification toward purchase and retention.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: For example, in a customer opportunity, an engineer may run into this concept when a sales funnel represents stages through which prospects progress from awareness or qualification toward purchase and retention. The implementation can then be checked against the expected behavior.\n4. Finish with the limitation: For Funnel, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a customer opportunity, an engineer may run into this concept when a sales funnel represents stages through which prospects progress from awareness or qualification toward purchase and retention. The implementation can then be checked against the expected behavior.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Funnel when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for example, in a customer opportunity, an engineer may run into this concept when a sales funnel represents stages through which prospects progress from awareness or qualification toward purchase and retention. The implementation can then be checked against the expected behavior. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Funnel as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Funnel.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
