    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Sales interview question about Qualification.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Qualification misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Qualification. Qualification determines whether an opportunity has a credible need, fit, authority, timing, and path to purchase.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Qualification: Qualification determines whether an opportunity has a credible need, fit, authority, timing, and path to purchase.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Use discovery questions to confirm business need, fit, decision process, timing, and a credible path to value before forecasting the opportunity.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: A more disciplined process improves focus and forecast quality but can slow conversations if it is applied as a rigid checklist instead of a decision aid.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nUse discovery questions to confirm business need, fit, decision process, timing, and a credible path to value before forecasting the opportunity.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Qualification when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use discovery questions to confirm business need, fit, decision process, timing, and a credible path to value before forecasting the opportunity. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Qualification as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Qualification.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Qualification misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Qualification. Qualification determines whether an opportunity has a credible need, fit, authority, timing, and path to purchase.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Qualification: Qualification determines whether an opportunity has a credible need, fit, authority, timing, and path to purchase.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Use discovery questions to confirm business need, fit, decision process, timing, and a credible path to value before forecasting the opportunity.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: A more disciplined process improves focus and forecast quality but can slow conversations if it is applied as a rigid checklist instead of a decision aid.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nUse discovery questions to confirm business need, fit, decision process, timing, and a credible path to value before forecasting the opportunity.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Qualification when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use discovery questions to confirm business need, fit, decision process, timing, and a credible path to value before forecasting the opportunity. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Qualification as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Qualification.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
