    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nQualification is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. A more disciplined process improves focus and forecast quality but can slow conversations if it is applied as a rigid checklist instead of a decision aid. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Qualification gives you: Qualification determines whether an opportunity has a credible need, fit, authority, timing, and path to purchase.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: A more disciplined process improves focus and forecast quality but can slow conversations if it is applied as a rigid checklist instead of a decision aid.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Use discovery questions to confirm business need, fit, decision process, timing, and a credible path to value before forecasting the opportunity.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nUse discovery questions to confirm business need, fit, decision process, timing, and a credible path to value before forecasting the opportunity.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Qualification when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use discovery questions to confirm business need, fit, decision process, timing, and a credible path to value before forecasting the opportunity. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Qualification as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Qualification.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
