    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Sales interview question about MEDDIC.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen MEDDIC misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to MEDDIC. MEDDIC is a structured qualification framework covering metrics, economic buyer, decision criteria/process, pain, champion, and competition.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls MEDDIC: MEDDIC is a structured qualification framework covering metrics, economic buyer, decision criteria/process, pain, champion, and competition.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: For a complex deal, identify measurable outcomes, decision criteria, decision process, pain, champion, and competition so the opportunity is evidence-backed.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For MEDDIC, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a complex deal, identify measurable outcomes, decision criteria, decision process, pain, champion, and competition so the opportunity is evidence-backed.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use MEDDIC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a complex deal, identify measurable outcomes, decision criteria, decision process, pain, champion, and competition so the opportunity is evidence-backed. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating MEDDIC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with MEDDIC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
