    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain MEDDIC in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: For a complex deal, identify measurable outcomes, decision criteria, decision process, pain, champion, and competition so the opportunity is evidence-backed.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: MEDDIC is a structured qualification framework covering metrics, economic buyer, decision criteria/process, pain, champion, and competition.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: For a complex deal, identify measurable outcomes, decision criteria, decision process, pain, champion, and competition so the opportunity is evidence-backed.\n4. Finish with the limitation: For MEDDIC, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a complex deal, identify measurable outcomes, decision criteria, decision process, pain, champion, and competition so the opportunity is evidence-backed.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use MEDDIC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a complex deal, identify measurable outcomes, decision criteria, decision process, pain, champion, and competition so the opportunity is evidence-backed. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating MEDDIC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with MEDDIC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
