    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Sales interview question about MEDDIC.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about MEDDIC when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to MEDDIC.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: MEDDIC is a structured qualification framework covering metrics, economic buyer, decision criteria/process, pain, champion, and competition.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: For a complex deal, identify measurable outcomes, decision criteria, decision process, pain, champion, and competition so the opportunity is evidence-backed.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For MEDDIC, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a complex deal, identify measurable outcomes, decision criteria, decision process, pain, champion, and competition so the opportunity is evidence-backed.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use MEDDIC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a complex deal, identify measurable outcomes, decision criteria, decision process, pain, champion, and competition so the opportunity is evidence-backed. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating MEDDIC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with MEDDIC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
