    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Sales interview question about Discovery.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Discovery misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Discovery. Discovery gathers information about the customer situation, goals, constraints, and decision process.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Discovery: Discovery gathers information about the customer situation, goals, constraints, and decision process.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Ask open questions about the customer's current process and impact before proposing a solution.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Discovery, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nAsk open questions about the customer's current process and impact before proposing a solution.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Discovery when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, ask open questions about the customer's current process and impact before proposing a solution. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Discovery as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Discovery.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
