    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Sales interview question about Value proposition.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA value proposition explains the customer outcome or benefit created by the product or service for a specific need.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Value proposition accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: A value proposition explains the customer outcome or benefit created by the product or service for a specific need.\n2. Use a concrete case: Connect the product capability to a specific customer outcome such as lower processing time or reduced operational effort.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Value proposition, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Value proposition first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nConnect the product capability to a specific customer outcome such as lower processing time or reduced operational effort.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Value proposition when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, connect the product capability to a specific customer outcome such as lower processing time or reduced operational effort. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Value proposition as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Value proposition.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Value proposition, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
