    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Value proposition comes from the behavior it provides: A value proposition explains the customer outcome or benefit created by the product or service for a specific need. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Connect the product capability to a specific customer outcome such as lower processing time or reduced operational effort.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: A value proposition explains the customer outcome or benefit created by the product or service for a specific need.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Connect the product capability to a specific customer outcome such as lower processing time or reduced operational effort.\n3. State the trade-off: For Value proposition, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nConnect the product capability to a specific customer outcome such as lower processing time or reduced operational effort.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Value proposition when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, connect the product capability to a specific customer outcome such as lower processing time or reduced operational effort. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Value proposition as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Value proposition.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Value proposition comes from the behavior it provides: A value proposition explains the customer outcome or benefit created by the product or service for a specific need. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Connect the product capability to a specific customer outcome such as lower processing time or reduced operational effort.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: A value proposition explains the customer outcome or benefit created by the product or service for a specific need.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Connect the product capability to a specific customer outcome such as lower processing time or reduced operational effort.\n3. State the trade-off: For Value proposition, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nConnect the product capability to a specific customer outcome such as lower processing time or reduced operational effort.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Value proposition when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, connect the product capability to a specific customer outcome such as lower processing time or reduced operational effort. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Value proposition as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Value proposition.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
