    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Value proposition are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: A value proposition explains the customer outcome or benefit created by the product or service for a specific need.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Treating Value proposition as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Value proposition.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: Connect the product capability to a specific customer outcome such as lower processing time or reduced operational effort.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nConnect the product capability to a specific customer outcome such as lower processing time or reduced operational effort.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Value proposition when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, connect the product capability to a specific customer outcome such as lower processing time or reduced operational effort. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Value proposition as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Value proposition.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Value proposition. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
