    slug: "sales-how-would-you-troubleshoot-a-problem-involving-pipeline-137",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Sales interview question about Pipeline.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Pipeline misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Pipeline. A sales pipeline is the set of active opportunities and their stages, values, and expected next steps.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Pipeline: A sales pipeline is the set of active opportunities and their stages, values, and expected next steps.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Review pipeline by stage, age, next step, and evidence so weak opportunities are not treated the same as committed deals.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: A more disciplined process improves focus and forecast quality but can slow conversations if it is applied as a rigid checklist instead of a decision aid.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nReview pipeline by stage, age, next step, and evidence so weak opportunities are not treated the same as committed deals.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Pipeline when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, review pipeline by stage, age, next step, and evidence so weak opportunities are not treated the same as committed deals. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Pipeline as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Pipeline.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Pipeline misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Pipeline. A sales pipeline is the set of active opportunities and their stages, values, and expected next steps.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Pipeline: A sales pipeline is the set of active opportunities and their stages, values, and expected next steps.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Review pipeline by stage, age, next step, and evidence so weak opportunities are not treated the same as committed deals.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: A more disciplined process improves focus and forecast quality but can slow conversations if it is applied as a rigid checklist instead of a decision aid.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nReview pipeline by stage, age, next step, and evidence so weak opportunities are not treated the same as committed deals.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Pipeline when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, review pipeline by stage, age, next step, and evidence so weak opportunities are not treated the same as committed deals. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Pipeline as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Pipeline.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
