    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPipeline is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. A more disciplined process improves focus and forecast quality but can slow conversations if it is applied as a rigid checklist instead of a decision aid. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Pipeline gives you: A sales pipeline is the set of active opportunities and their stages, values, and expected next steps.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: A more disciplined process improves focus and forecast quality but can slow conversations if it is applied as a rigid checklist instead of a decision aid.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Review pipeline by stage, age, next step, and evidence so weak opportunities are not treated the same as committed deals.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nReview pipeline by stage, age, next step, and evidence so weak opportunities are not treated the same as committed deals.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Pipeline when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, review pipeline by stage, age, next step, and evidence so weak opportunities are not treated the same as committed deals. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Pipeline as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Pipeline.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
