    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Sales interview question about Forecasting.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSales forecasting estimates likely future bookings or revenue from pipeline and historical evidence.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Forecasting accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Sales forecasting estimates likely future bookings or revenue from pipeline and historical evidence.\n2. Use a concrete case: Forecast an opportunity from evidence such as decision stage, customer actions, timeline, and known risks rather than optimism.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: A more disciplined process improves focus and forecast quality but can slow conversations if it is applied as a rigid checklist instead of a decision aid.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Forecasting first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nForecast an opportunity from evidence such as decision stage, customer actions, timeline, and known risks rather than optimism.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Forecasting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, forecast an opportunity from evidence such as decision stage, customer actions, timeline, and known risks rather than optimism. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Forecasting as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Forecasting.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Forecasting, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
