    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Sales interview question about Forecasting.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Forecasting comes from the behavior it provides: Sales forecasting estimates likely future bookings or revenue from pipeline and historical evidence. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Forecast an opportunity from evidence such as decision stage, customer actions, timeline, and known risks rather than optimism.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Sales forecasting estimates likely future bookings or revenue from pipeline and historical evidence.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Forecast an opportunity from evidence such as decision stage, customer actions, timeline, and known risks rather than optimism.\n3. State the trade-off: A more disciplined process improves focus and forecast quality but can slow conversations if it is applied as a rigid checklist instead of a decision aid.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nForecast an opportunity from evidence such as decision stage, customer actions, timeline, and known risks rather than optimism.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Forecasting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, forecast an opportunity from evidence such as decision stage, customer actions, timeline, and known risks rather than optimism. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Forecasting as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Forecasting.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
