    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Forecasting misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Forecasting. Sales forecasting estimates likely future bookings or revenue from pipeline and historical evidence.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Forecasting: Sales forecasting estimates likely future bookings or revenue from pipeline and historical evidence.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Forecast an opportunity from evidence such as decision stage, customer actions, timeline, and known risks rather than optimism.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: A more disciplined process improves focus and forecast quality but can slow conversations if it is applied as a rigid checklist instead of a decision aid.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nForecast an opportunity from evidence such as decision stage, customer actions, timeline, and known risks rather than optimism.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Forecasting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, forecast an opportunity from evidence such as decision stage, customer actions, timeline, and known risks rather than optimism. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Forecasting as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Forecasting.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
