    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Sales interview question about Negotiation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nNegotiation balances customer and seller interests to reach an acceptable agreement on scope, price, risk, and terms.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Negotiation accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Negotiation balances customer and seller interests to reach an acceptable agreement on scope, price, risk, and terms.\n2. Use a concrete case: Trade concrete terms deliberately, such as price, scope, implementation timing, or support, instead of conceding everything at once.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Negotiation, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Negotiation first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nTrade concrete terms deliberately, such as price, scope, implementation timing, or support, instead of conceding everything at once.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Negotiation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, trade concrete terms deliberately, such as price, scope, implementation timing, or support, instead of conceding everything at once. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Negotiation as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Negotiation.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Negotiation, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
