    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Sales interview question about Negotiation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Negotiation comes from the behavior it provides: Negotiation balances customer and seller interests to reach an acceptable agreement on scope, price, risk, and terms. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Trade concrete terms deliberately, such as price, scope, implementation timing, or support, instead of conceding everything at once.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Negotiation balances customer and seller interests to reach an acceptable agreement on scope, price, risk, and terms.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Trade concrete terms deliberately, such as price, scope, implementation timing, or support, instead of conceding everything at once.\n3. State the trade-off: For Negotiation, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nTrade concrete terms deliberately, such as price, scope, implementation timing, or support, instead of conceding everything at once.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Negotiation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, trade concrete terms deliberately, such as price, scope, implementation timing, or support, instead of conceding everything at once. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Negotiation as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Negotiation.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
