    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Retention are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Retention is maintaining customer value and reducing avoidable churn after purchase.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Treating Retention as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Retention.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: Keep events for the replay period the business needs instead of retaining everything forever.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nKeep events for the replay period the business needs instead of retaining everything forever.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use Retention when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, keep events for the replay period the business needs instead of retaining everything forever. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Retention as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Retention.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Retention. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
