    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about CRM metrics when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to CRM metrics.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: CRM metrics measure sales activity and outcomes such as conversion, cycle time, pipeline coverage, win rate, and retention.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Use conversion, cycle time, stage aging, and win/loss data to identify where the sales process needs attention.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For CRM metrics, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nUse conversion, cycle time, stage aging, and win/loss data to identify where the sales process needs attention.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA customer opportunity may use CRM metrics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use conversion, cycle time, stage aging, and win/loss data to identify where the sales process needs attention. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating CRM metrics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with CRM metrics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
