    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for OOP is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Object-oriented programming organizes behavior and state around objects and types, using concepts such as encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, and polymorphism.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Object-oriented programming organizes behavior and state around objects and types, using concepts such as encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, and polymorphism.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A checkout system can model an `Order` object while keeping payment behavior behind an interface, so the calling code does not depend on one payment provider implementation.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: For OOP, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA checkout system can model an `Order` object while keeping payment behavior behind an interface, so the calling code does not depend on one payment provider implementation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use OOP when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a checkout system can model an `Order` object while keeping payment behavior behind an interface, so the calling code does not depend on one payment provider implementation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating OOP as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with OOP.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
