    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen OOP misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to OOP. Object-oriented programming organizes behavior and state around objects and types, using concepts such as encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, and polymorphism.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls OOP: Object-oriented programming organizes behavior and state around objects and types, using concepts such as encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, and polymorphism.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A checkout system can model an `Order` object while keeping payment behavior behind an interface, so the calling code does not depend on one payment provider implementation.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For OOP, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA checkout system can model an `Order` object while keeping payment behavior behind an interface, so the calling code does not depend on one payment provider implementation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use OOP when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a checkout system can model an `Order` object while keeping payment behavior behind an interface, so the calling code does not depend on one payment provider implementation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating OOP as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with OOP.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen OOP misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to OOP. Object-oriented programming organizes behavior and state around objects and types, using concepts such as encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, and polymorphism.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls OOP: Object-oriented programming organizes behavior and state around objects and types, using concepts such as encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, and polymorphism.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A checkout system can model an `Order` object while keeping payment behavior behind an interface, so the calling code does not depend on one payment provider implementation.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For OOP, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA checkout system can model an `Order` object while keeping payment behavior behind an interface, so the calling code does not depend on one payment provider implementation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use OOP when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a checkout system can model an `Order` object while keeping payment behavior behind an interface, so the calling code does not depend on one payment provider implementation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating OOP as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with OOP.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
