    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software engineer Fresher-internship interview question about OOP.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nOOP is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For OOP, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what OOP gives you: Object-oriented programming organizes behavior and state around objects and types, using concepts such as encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, and polymorphism.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For OOP, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A checkout system can model an `Order` object while keeping payment behavior behind an interface, so the calling code does not depend on one payment provider implementation.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA checkout system can model an `Order` object while keeping payment behavior behind an interface, so the calling code does not depend on one payment provider implementation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use OOP when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a checkout system can model an `Order` object while keeping payment behavior behind an interface, so the calling code does not depend on one payment provider implementation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating OOP as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with OOP.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
