    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Inheritance is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Inheritance lets a type derive behavior or structure from another type when an “is-a” relationship is appropriate.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Inheritance lets a type derive behavior or structure from another type when an “is-a” relationship is appropriate.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A domain model might extend a common base type for truly shared behavior, but unrelated classes should not be forced into the same hierarchy merely to reuse code.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: For Inheritance, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA domain model might extend a common base type for truly shared behavior, but unrelated classes should not be forced into the same hierarchy merely to reuse code.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use Inheritance when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a domain model might extend a common base type for truly shared behavior, but unrelated classes should not be forced into the same hierarchy merely to reuse code. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Inheritance as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Inheritance.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
