    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Inheritance comes from the behavior it provides: Inheritance lets a type derive behavior or structure from another type when an “is-a” relationship is appropriate. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A domain model might extend a common base type for truly shared behavior, but unrelated classes should not be forced into the same hierarchy merely to reuse code.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Inheritance lets a type derive behavior or structure from another type when an “is-a” relationship is appropriate.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A domain model might extend a common base type for truly shared behavior, but unrelated classes should not be forced into the same hierarchy merely to reuse code.\n3. State the trade-off: For Inheritance, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA domain model might extend a common base type for truly shared behavior, but unrelated classes should not be forced into the same hierarchy merely to reuse code.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use Inheritance when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a domain model might extend a common base type for truly shared behavior, but unrelated classes should not be forced into the same hierarchy merely to reuse code. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Inheritance as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Inheritance.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
