    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Inheritance misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Inheritance. Inheritance lets a type derive behavior or structure from another type when an “is-a” relationship is appropriate.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Inheritance: Inheritance lets a type derive behavior or structure from another type when an “is-a” relationship is appropriate.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A domain model might extend a common base type for truly shared behavior, but unrelated classes should not be forced into the same hierarchy merely to reuse code.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Inheritance, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA domain model might extend a common base type for truly shared behavior, but unrelated classes should not be forced into the same hierarchy merely to reuse code.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use Inheritance when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a domain model might extend a common base type for truly shared behavior, but unrelated classes should not be forced into the same hierarchy merely to reuse code. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Inheritance as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Inheritance.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
