    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nInheritance is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Inheritance, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Inheritance gives you: Inheritance lets a type derive behavior or structure from another type when an “is-a” relationship is appropriate.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Inheritance, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A domain model might extend a common base type for truly shared behavior, but unrelated classes should not be forced into the same hierarchy merely to reuse code.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA domain model might extend a common base type for truly shared behavior, but unrelated classes should not be forced into the same hierarchy merely to reuse code.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use Inheritance when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a domain model might extend a common base type for truly shared behavior, but unrelated classes should not be forced into the same hierarchy merely to reuse code. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Inheritance as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Inheritance.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
