    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software engineer Fresher-internship interview question about Composition.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Composition comes from the behavior it provides: Composition builds an object from collaborating objects and is often preferred when behavior should be assembled rather than inherited. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A checkout service can receive a tax calculator and payment gateway as collaborators instead of inheriting from them; the service is assembled from parts with separate responsibilities.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Composition builds an object from collaborating objects and is often preferred when behavior should be assembled rather than inherited.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A checkout service can receive a tax calculator and payment gateway as collaborators instead of inheriting from them; the service is assembled from parts with separate responsibilities.\n3. State the trade-off: For Composition, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA checkout service can receive a tax calculator and payment gateway as collaborators instead of inheriting from them; the service is assembled from parts with separate responsibilities.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use Composition when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a checkout service can receive a tax calculator and payment gateway as collaborators instead of inheriting from them; the service is assembled from parts with separate responsibilities. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Composition as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Composition.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
