    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software engineer Fresher-internship interview question about Composition.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Composition misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Composition. Composition builds an object from collaborating objects and is often preferred when behavior should be assembled rather than inherited.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Composition: Composition builds an object from collaborating objects and is often preferred when behavior should be assembled rather than inherited.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A checkout service can receive a tax calculator and payment gateway as collaborators instead of inheriting from them; the service is assembled from parts with separate responsibilities.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Composition, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA checkout service can receive a tax calculator and payment gateway as collaborators instead of inheriting from them; the service is assembled from parts with separate responsibilities.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use Composition when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a checkout service can receive a tax calculator and payment gateway as collaborators instead of inheriting from them; the service is assembled from parts with separate responsibilities. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Composition as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Composition.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
