    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nComposition is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Composition, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Composition gives you: Composition builds an object from collaborating objects and is often preferred when behavior should be assembled rather than inherited.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Composition, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A checkout service can receive a tax calculator and payment gateway as collaborators instead of inheriting from them; the service is assembled from parts with separate responsibilities.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA checkout service can receive a tax calculator and payment gateway as collaborators instead of inheriting from them; the service is assembled from parts with separate responsibilities.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use Composition when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a checkout service can receive a tax calculator and payment gateway as collaborators instead of inheriting from them; the service is assembled from parts with separate responsibilities. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Composition as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Composition.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
