    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Interfaces is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. An interface defines a contract or capability that implementing types agree to provide.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: An interface defines a contract or capability that implementing types agree to provide.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A notification service can depend on a `NotificationSender` interface, allowing email and SMS implementations to be substituted without changing the business logic.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: For Interfaces, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA notification service can depend on a `NotificationSender` interface, allowing email and SMS implementations to be substituted without changing the business logic.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use Interfaces when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a notification service can depend on a `NotificationSender` interface, allowing email and SMS implementations to be substituted without changing the business logic. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Interfaces as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Interfaces.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
