    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for LinkedList is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. LinkedList stores elements as linked nodes and can support efficient insertion/removal when the relevant node is already known, but indexed access is linear.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: LinkedList stores elements as linked nodes and can support efficient insertion/removal when the relevant node is already known, but indexed access is linear.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: When a Java program genuinely needs frequent insertion or removal through a linked-list iterator, `LinkedList` can represent that sequence; for many queue/deque workloads, `ArrayDeque` is a better fit.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: For LinkedList, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nWhen a Java program genuinely needs frequent insertion or removal through a linked-list iterator, `LinkedList` can represent that sequence; for many queue/deque workloads, `ArrayDeque` is a better fit.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use LinkedList when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, when a Java program genuinely needs frequent insertion or removal through a linked-list iterator, `LinkedList` can represent that sequence; for many queue/deque workloads, `ArrayDeque` is a better fit. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating LinkedList as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with LinkedList.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
