    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of HashMap comes from the behavior it provides: HashMap stores key-value associations using hashing and provides expected constant-time lookup under normal conditions. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A user service can keep a `HashMap<Long, User>` for fast in-memory lookup by user ID when the data set is appropriately sized and safely scoped for concurrent access.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: HashMap stores key-value associations using hashing and provides expected constant-time lookup under normal conditions.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A user service can keep a `HashMap<Long, User>` for fast in-memory lookup by user ID when the data set is appropriately sized and safely scoped for concurrent access.\n3. State the trade-off: For HashMap, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA user service can keep a `HashMap<Long, User>` for fast in-memory lookup by user ID when the data set is appropriately sized and safely scoped for concurrent access.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use HashMap when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a user service can keep a `HashMap<Long, User>` for fast in-memory lookup by user ID when the data set is appropriately sized and safely scoped for concurrent access. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Ignoring key equality/hash behavior.\n- Assuming average O(1) means no worst-case or resizing cost.\n- Using a non-thread-safe map as shared mutable state without a concurrency strategy.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
