    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHashMap is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For HashMap, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what HashMap gives you: HashMap stores key-value associations using hashing and provides expected constant-time lookup under normal conditions.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For HashMap, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A user service can keep a `HashMap<Long, User>` for fast in-memory lookup by user ID when the data set is appropriately sized and safely scoped for concurrent access.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA user service can keep a `HashMap<Long, User>` for fast in-memory lookup by user ID when the data set is appropriately sized and safely scoped for concurrent access.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use HashMap when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a user service can keep a `HashMap<Long, User>` for fast in-memory lookup by user ID when the data set is appropriately sized and safely scoped for concurrent access. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Ignoring key equality/hash behavior.\n- Assuming average O(1) means no worst-case or resizing cost.\n- Using a non-thread-safe map as shared mutable state without a concurrency strategy.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
