    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSQL is the language used to query and manipulate data in relational database systems.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define SQL accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: SQL is the language used to query and manipulate data in relational database systems.\n2. Use a concrete case: An order service can query only the columns it needs, filter by customer ID, and use a parameterized query rather than concatenating untrusted input.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Faster reads can come with extra storage, write overhead, or plan complexity, so the access pattern should justify the change.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define SQL first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAn order service can query only the columns it needs, filter by customer ID, and use a parameterized query rather than concatenating untrusted input.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use SQL when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an order service can query only the columns it needs, filter by customer ID, and use a parameterized query rather than concatenating untrusted input. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor SQL, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
