    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software engineer Fresher-internship interview question about SQL.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen SQL misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to SQL. SQL is the language used to query and manipulate data in relational database systems.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls SQL: SQL is the language used to query and manipulate data in relational database systems.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: An order service can query only the columns it needs, filter by customer ID, and use a parameterized query rather than concatenating untrusted input.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Faster reads can come with extra storage, write overhead, or plan complexity, so the access pattern should justify the change.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nAn order service can query only the columns it needs, filter by customer ID, and use a parameterized query rather than concatenating untrusted input.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use SQL when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an order service can query only the columns it needs, filter by customer ID, and use a parameterized query rather than concatenating untrusted input. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
