    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software engineer Fresher-internship interview question about SQL.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain SQL in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: An order service can query only the columns it needs, filter by customer ID, and use a parameterized query rather than concatenating untrusted input.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: SQL is the language used to query and manipulate data in relational database systems.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: An order service can query only the columns it needs, filter by customer ID, and use a parameterized query rather than concatenating untrusted input.\n4. Finish with the limitation: Faster reads can come with extra storage, write overhead, or plan complexity, so the access pattern should justify the change.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nAn order service can query only the columns it needs, filter by customer ID, and use a parameterized query rather than concatenating untrusted input.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use SQL when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an order service can query only the columns it needs, filter by customer ID, and use a parameterized query rather than concatenating untrusted input. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
