    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Joins are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: SQL joins combine rows from related tables according to a join condition.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Treating Joins as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Joins.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: A reporting query can join `orders` to `customers` on the customer ID to return order totals together with customer information.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nA reporting query can join `orders` to `customers` on the customer ID to return order totals together with customer information.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use Joins when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a reporting query can join `orders` to `customers` on the customer ID to return order totals together with customer information. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Joins as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Joins.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Joins. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
