    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nREST is an architectural style commonly applied to HTTP APIs using resources, representations, stateless requests, and standard HTTP semantics.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define REST accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: REST is an architectural style commonly applied to HTTP APIs using resources, representations, stateless requests, and standard HTTP semantics.\n2. Use a concrete case: A client can call `GET /users/42` to retrieve a user resource and receive a documented status code and representation.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For REST, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define REST first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA client can call `GET /users/42` to retrieve a user resource and receive a documented status code and representation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use REST when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a client can call `GET /users/42` to retrieve a user resource and receive a documented status code and representation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor REST, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
