    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software engineer Fresher-internship interview question about Git.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nGit is a distributed version-control system that records project history and supports branching and merging.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Git accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Git is a distributed version-control system that records project history and supports branching and merging.\n2. Use a concrete case: A developer can create a focused feature branch, commit a small change, push it for review, and merge it after CI passes.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Git, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Git first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA developer can create a focused feature branch, commit a small change, push it for review, and merge it after CI passes.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use Git when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a developer can create a focused feature branch, commit a small change, push it for review, and merge it after CI passes. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Git as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Git.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Git, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
