    slug: "software-engineer-fresher-internship-how-does-unit-testing-work-183",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software engineer Fresher-internship interview question about Unit testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Unit testing is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Unit testing verifies a small piece of code in isolation from unrelated infrastructure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Unit testing verifies a small piece of code in isolation from unrelated infrastructure.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Broader coverage can improve confidence, but execution time, flakiness, and maintenance cost grow if checks are added without regard to risk.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use Unit testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
