    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “How would you keep Unit testing reliable as the product and test suite grow?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Unit testing.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Unit testing verifies a small piece of code in isolation from unrelated infrastructure.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Broader coverage can improve confidence, but execution time, flakiness, and maintenance cost grow if checks are added without regard to risk.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use Unit testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
