    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software engineer Fresher-internship interview question about Debugging.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDebugging is the process of reproducing a problem, isolating its cause, and verifying the fix.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Debugging accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Debugging is the process of reproducing a problem, isolating its cause, and verifying the fix.\n2. Use a concrete case: If an API starts returning 500 errors, an engineer can reproduce the request, inspect the stack trace and logs, identify the failing layer, and verify the fix with a regression test.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Debugging, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Debugging first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nIf an API starts returning 500 errors, an engineer can reproduce the request, inspect the stack trace and logs, identify the failing layer, and verify the fix with a regression test.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use Debugging when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if an API starts returning 500 errors, an engineer can reproduce the request, inspect the stack trace and logs, identify the failing layer, and verify the fix with a regression test. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Debugging as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Debugging.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Debugging, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
