    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Debugging misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Debugging. Debugging is the process of reproducing a problem, isolating its cause, and verifying the fix.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Debugging: Debugging is the process of reproducing a problem, isolating its cause, and verifying the fix.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: If an API starts returning 500 errors, an engineer can reproduce the request, inspect the stack trace and logs, identify the failing layer, and verify the fix with a regression test.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Debugging, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nIf an API starts returning 500 errors, an engineer can reproduce the request, inspect the stack trace and logs, identify the failing layer, and verify the fix with a regression test.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application being built or maintained by a junior engineer may use Debugging when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if an API starts returning 500 errors, an engineer can reproduce the request, inspect the stack trace and logs, identify the failing layer, and verify the fix with a regression test. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Debugging as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Debugging.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
