    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because maintainable code is organized so that future engineers can understand, change, test, and extend it with reasonable effort. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: instead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Maintainable code addresses it: Maintainable code is organized so that future engineers can understand, change, test, and extend it with reasonable effort.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: Instead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: For Maintainable code, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nInstead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Maintainable code when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, instead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Maintainable code as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Maintainable code.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
