    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Maintainable code comes from the behavior it provides: Maintainable code is organized so that future engineers can understand, change, test, and extend it with reasonable effort. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Instead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Maintainable code is organized so that future engineers can understand, change, test, and extend it with reasonable effort.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Instead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs.\n3. State the trade-off: For Maintainable code, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nInstead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Maintainable code when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, instead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Maintainable code as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Maintainable code.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
