    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Maintainable code misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Maintainable code. Maintainable code is organized so that future engineers can understand, change, test, and extend it with reasonable effort.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Maintainable code: Maintainable code is organized so that future engineers can understand, change, test, and extend it with reasonable effort.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Instead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Maintainable code, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nInstead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Maintainable code when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, instead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Maintainable code as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Maintainable code.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Maintainable code misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Maintainable code. Maintainable code is organized so that future engineers can understand, change, test, and extend it with reasonable effort.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Maintainable code: Maintainable code is organized so that future engineers can understand, change, test, and extend it with reasonable effort.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Instead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Maintainable code, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nInstead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Maintainable code when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, instead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Maintainable code as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Maintainable code.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    explanation: "How would you troubleshoot a problem involving Maintainable code? is a core Software engineer 1-2 Years of expirance interview concept. A strong answer should define the concept clearly, explain how it works, show where it is useful, and discuss practical trade-offs. The candidate should connect the explanation to a realistic engineering or business scenario and distinguish the concept from closely related alternatives.",
