    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software engineer 1-2 Years of expirance interview question about Maintainable code.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMaintainable code is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Maintainable code, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Maintainable code gives you: Maintainable code is organized so that future engineers can understand, change, test, and extend it with reasonable effort.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Maintainable code, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Instead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nInstead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Maintainable code when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, instead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Maintainable code as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Maintainable code.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
