    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Maintainable code when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Maintainable code.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Maintainable code is organized so that future engineers can understand, change, test, and extend it with reasonable effort.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Instead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Maintainable code, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nInstead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Maintainable code when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, instead of one 300-line method that validates, persists, and formats data, split the responsibilities into smaller units with explicit inputs and outputs. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Maintainable code as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Maintainable code.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
