    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software engineer 1-2 Years of expirance interview question about SOLID.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSOLID is a group of object-oriented design principles covering responsibilities, extension, substitutability, interface design, and dependency direction.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define SOLID accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: SOLID is a group of object-oriented design principles covering responsibilities, extension, substitutability, interface design, and dependency direction.\n2. Use a concrete case: A service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For SOLID, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define SOLID first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use SOLID when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating SOLID as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with SOLID.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor SOLID, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
