    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because sOLID is a group of object-oriented design principles covering responsibilities, extension, substitutability, interface design, and dependency direction. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: a service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how SOLID addresses it: SOLID is a group of object-oriented design principles covering responsibilities, extension, substitutability, interface design, and dependency direction.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: A service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: For SOLID, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nA service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use SOLID when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating SOLID as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with SOLID.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because sOLID is a group of object-oriented design principles covering responsibilities, extension, substitutability, interface design, and dependency direction. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: a service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how SOLID addresses it: SOLID is a group of object-oriented design principles covering responsibilities, extension, substitutability, interface design, and dependency direction.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: A service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: For SOLID, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nA service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use SOLID when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating SOLID as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with SOLID.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
