    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for SOLID is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. SOLID is a group of object-oriented design principles covering responsibilities, extension, substitutability, interface design, and dependency direction.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: SOLID is a group of object-oriented design principles covering responsibilities, extension, substitutability, interface design, and dependency direction.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: For SOLID, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use SOLID when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating SOLID as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with SOLID.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
