    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen SOLID misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to SOLID. SOLID is a group of object-oriented design principles covering responsibilities, extension, substitutability, interface design, and dependency direction.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls SOLID: SOLID is a group of object-oriented design principles covering responsibilities, extension, substitutability, interface design, and dependency direction.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For SOLID, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use SOLID when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service that depends on an interface rather than a concrete payment gateway can be tested with a fake gateway and changed without rewriting its business logic. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating SOLID as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with SOLID.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
